WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.936
A payment gateway holds 10,000 open connections from merchants.

2
00:00:06.936 --> 00:00:09.962
At any moment, only a few have sent anything.

3
00:00:09.962 --> 00:00:11.979
How many threads does it need?

4
00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:15.733
The simplest design gives each connection its own thread.

5
00:00:15.733 --> 00:00:20.320
The thread calls read, and the kernel puts it to sleep until bytes arrive.

6
00:00:20.533 --> 00:00:24.177
When a request comes in, the kernel wakes that one thread.

7
00:00:24.177 --> 00:00:26.691
It reads, answers, and calls read again.

8
00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:35.607
It works, but in the lab, 2,000 idle clients took 2,028 threads and about 200 megabytes, almost all of it asleep.

9
00:00:35.607 --> 00:00:40.076
At 10,000, every thread's stack and every wake-up adds up.

10
00:00:40.233 --> 00:00:45.381
What we want is one thread that waits on all of them, and is told which ones have data.

11
00:00:45.567 --> 00:00:48.320
Asking each socket in turn doesn't work.

12
00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:54.860
A non-blocking read on a quiet socket fails at once, so the loop burns a whole CPU on failures.

13
00:00:55.033 --> 00:00:59.882
The older calls, select and poll, hand the kernel the whole list every time.

14
00:00:59.882 --> 00:01:04.348
The kernel checks all 10,000 sockets to find the three that are ready.

15
00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:06.109
epoll splits that in two.

16
00:01:06.109 --> 00:01:10.551
The thread registers each socket once, and the kernel keeps the list.

17
00:01:10.733 --> 00:01:15.356
When bytes arrive, the kernel itself puts that socket on a ready list.

18
00:01:15.356 --> 00:01:16.677
Nobody had to check.

19
00:01:16.867 --> 00:01:18.544
The thread calls epoll wait.

20
00:01:18.544 --> 00:01:23.216
It sleeps until the ready list has something, then gets back just those three.

21
00:01:23.367 --> 00:01:26.125
It reads each one, answers, and asks again.

22
00:01:26.125 --> 00:01:28.948
Quiet connections cost nothing on each call.

23
00:01:29.133 --> 00:01:38.271
In the lab, a Selector server, which is epoll underneath, served the same 2,000 clients with 19 threads and 51 megabytes.

24
00:01:38.467 --> 00:01:43.487
Netty, the Kafka client and Java's virtual threads all run this loop for you.

25
00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:46.943
A thread per connection pays for every connection.

26
00:01:46.943 --> 00:01:49.473
epoll pays only for the ones with data.
